        "heading": "What Is Tendinopathy and What Are Its Symptoms?",
        "body": "Tendinopathy is the broad term that covers any disorder of the tendon, whether chronic or acute, with or without pain. Tendinopathy occurs when the limit of elasticity is exceeded, or when the continuity of some of the collagen fibres within the tendon is disrupted. The symptoms of tendinopathy are:\n\n• A reduction in the strength of the muscle\n\n• Atrophy of the muscle in chronic conditions\n\n• It may be accompanied by pain, but in advanced stages this is not necessarily the case\n\nTendinopathy can be caused by:\n\n• A sudden, abrupt, heavy load that the tendon is not accustomed to bearing\n\n• A small load that is repeated continuously and overstrains it\n\n• A shear load — a force perpendicular to the tendon — that causes injury, which may result either from a direct blow or from chronic overuse"
